WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.334
This is the Enigma, 

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:04.754
the encryption machine
used by the Nazis in World War two.

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:06.506
I see that there is a sigil

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:09.718
from the Nazi Party on this one,
so it must be the real thing.

00:00:09.718 --> 00:00:12.804
Yes. This was actually used
by the Germans in World War II.

00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:16.558
Someone actually asked me the other
day, did Hitler use this machine?

00:00:16.558 --> 00:00:17.892
And I said, I don't know,
but

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:19.769
Benedict Cumberbatch and
Keira Knightley did.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:22.522
Woah, no way!
Could we try encrypting a word?

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.566
Yeah.
- I have just a word for us.

00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:27.402
You'd never guess what it is.
- So that's our plaintext.

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:28.695
That is our plaintext.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.239
So we need to press the keys.

00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:32.615
And look at the lamps

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:35.243
that light up,
which will give us our ciphertext.

00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:37.996
So the first one is V.

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:38.997
We have a J.

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:43.293
Okay. Then an E.
- F
- Then an R.

00:00:43.293 --> 00:00:44.794
We have an M.

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.340
That is an L.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.426
Now for
someone to decrypt this message

00:00:51.426 --> 00:00:54.596
they need to type this text
into another Enigma machine.

00:00:54.596 --> 00:00:57.015
But not just any enigma.

00:00:57.015 --> 00:00:58.224
Their machine has to be set up

00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:01.227
in the exact same way as the one
that encrypted the message.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.856
So we need to turn these rotors
back to the initial setting.

00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:07.609
That means adjusting
a series of rotors and wires

00:01:07.609 --> 00:01:11.279
to exactly one of trillions
of possible combinations.

00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:12.947
If you get it right....

00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:15.617
Finally, the L gives us:
- Veritasium

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:17.452
It worked. Yeah.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:20.497
The message appears,
but if just a single setting

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:23.875
is out of place, well, then
the encryption remains unbroken.

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:26.252
I can see how decrypting
such a thing

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.379
would get very complicated
very quickly.

00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:30.924
This is exactly what the Nazis
relied on.

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.426
Hitler
had come into power in Germany

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.720
and the Nazi Party had taken over.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.597
Today we rule Germany.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.974
Tomorrow the world.

00:01:39.974 --> 00:01:42.936
By 1939, they were sending hundreds
of encrypted messages

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.437
across Europe every day.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:49.442
With the allies desperately trying
to guess the Enigma settings.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.862
But even if they sometimes
got lucky and cracked a message

00:01:52.862 --> 00:01:54.989
each day at midnight,
the Germans would change

00:01:54.989 --> 00:01:57.492
the settings
on all their Enigma machines,

00:01:57.492 --> 00:02:00.495
so none of it would help them read
the next day's messages.

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:05.792
That's why British intelligence assembled a team of the country's greatest minds:

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.628
chess players,
crossword fanatics and university

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:12.674
academics including Alan Turing,
along with hundreds of women

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:14.843
from the Royal Navy.
And bit by bit,

00:02:14.843 --> 00:02:18.054
they looked for flaws
hidden deep within the machine.

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:22.475
But they also searched for subtle
mistakes made by German operators.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:25.103
At its peak, around 9000
men and women

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:28.273
were executing the most secret code
breaking operations in the world.

00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:31.901
Out of an unsuspecting mansion
in the English countryside.

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:32.777
This is a video

00:02:32.777 --> 00:02:36.239
about the incredible methods
and machines that broke the enigma.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.868
Let's start
with how the Enigma actually works.

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:40.952
And I think the first thing

00:02:40.952 --> 00:02:44.205
you need to know about the Enigma
is that it wasn't actually invented

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:44.956
by the Nazis.

00:02:44.956 --> 00:02:49.127
It was patented in 1918
by a guy called Arthur Scherbius.

00:02:49.127 --> 00:02:52.172
He was a German inventor
who wanted to encrypt messages

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:54.090
for banks and businesses.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.176
Were Enigma machines
readily available?

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:56.676
Could you, like,

00:02:56.676 --> 00:03:00.513
buy one in Germany,
or were they kept a secret anyways?

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.724
This, by the way, is Sir
Dermot Turing.

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:04.642
He's Alan Turing's nephew.

00:03:04.642 --> 00:03:06.519
The company that was making Enigma

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.396
machines was really trying
to find a market for them,

00:03:08.396 --> 00:03:10.481
so you could definitely go out
and buy them.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:12.817
And lots of countries did.

00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:15.653
So Poland bought one,
the British bought one,

00:03:15.653 --> 00:03:17.363
the Russians bought one,
you name it.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:21.284
And there's one thing in particular
that made Enigma superior

00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:22.577
to other encryption methods.

00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:23.953
And you only need to play around

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:25.788
with it
for a bit to figure that out.

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:27.707
I'm going to press the L key.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:32.212
And when I do that,
you'll see that one of the lamps

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:36.633
on the lamp board lights up. 
The Y.

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:39.469
That means
that the plaintext letter L

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:42.639
has been encrypted as a Y.

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:46.601
If I press the L again,
what do you think might light up?

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:48.311
Maybe the Y again?
- Maybe the Y.

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:51.397
So let's press it again
and see what happens.

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:52.565
And what has lit up there.

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.151
Well that's an H not a Y.

00:03:55.151 --> 00:03:57.946
So it's not a Y. So it's changed.

00:03:57.946 --> 00:04:02.533
Okay, so with every key press
the substitution is different.
- Yes.

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:04.285
This is what separates the enigma

00:04:04.285 --> 00:04:05.954
from the most famous encryption
methods

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:09.707
that came before, the Caesar cipher
or simple substitution ciphers.

00:04:09.707 --> 00:04:12.627
These all encrypt
according to a fixed rule.

00:04:12.627 --> 00:04:16.839
Enigma changes that rule
with every single letter you type.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:19.676
And for the reason for that,
we need to open the box

00:04:19.676 --> 00:04:22.095
even further
and see what's happening inside.

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:23.346
Yeah, that'd be great.

00:04:23.346 --> 00:04:25.265
Okay, so I'm going to open this up.

00:04:26.557 --> 00:04:29.686
Inside the Enigma there are three
rotors.

00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:33.022
Each rotor has 26 metal contacts
on either side, one

00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:34.816
for each letter of the alphabet.

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:36.901
So when you slot them
into the machine,

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:38.945
current flows
from one rotor to the next,

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:42.907
the wiring between
the contacts is totally scrambled.

00:04:42.907 --> 00:04:46.160
So for example, when you type
a Y current flows down the

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.580
Y wire to the first rotor,
where it first gets switched to an

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.959
H, and then in the second rotor,
the H gets swapped to a B,

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.962
and in the third rotor
the B gets swapped to a q.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:58.256
Then a component called
the reflector directs

00:04:58.256 --> 00:04:59.590
the current back through the rotors

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.469
by a completely different path,
ending up as a letter E.

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.179
The current
then goes up to the lamps

00:05:05.179 --> 00:05:07.140
above the keyboard and illuminates
the letter

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.143
E, indicating how the
Y has been enciphered.

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:12.270
I just want to say a big
thanks to Jared Owen,

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.856
who made the 3D model of the Enigma
for his YouTube

00:05:14.856 --> 00:05:17.108
channel and has kindly
let us use it here.

00:05:17.108 --> 00:05:20.695
So check out the link in the
description for his original video.

00:05:20.695 --> 00:05:23.489
Now, the moment you type
a letter into the Enigma,

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:25.450
the rotor on the right rotates.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:30.121
And that's how a rotor encryption
machine works,

00:05:30.121 --> 00:05:33.875
that it actually moves the rotor
when you press the keys.

00:05:33.875 --> 00:05:35.752
So if you type
the same letter again,

00:05:35.752 --> 00:05:37.503
it will follow
a completely different path

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.714
through the machine,
which means you'll get

00:05:39.714 --> 00:05:43.009
a different letter
each time you press the key.

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:45.511
So here you can
see the rotors in position. Yes.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:49.974
And when I press the key
you can at the back

00:05:49.974 --> 00:05:54.604
see levers
which push up against the rotors.

00:05:54.604 --> 00:05:56.064
Could you press the button? Oh, I see.

00:05:56.064 --> 00:05:57.565
There we go.

00:05:57.565 --> 00:06:00.276
There's actually three levers.

00:06:00.276 --> 00:06:02.236
And they are set to the right hand

00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:04.655
side of each rotor.
So there's one for each rotor

00:06:04.655 --> 00:06:07.200
trying to push the three rotors
around.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.329
But it's only the first rotor that
turns every time you press a key.

00:06:11.329 --> 00:06:12.121
Right. Yeah.

00:06:12.121 --> 00:06:15.833
The second rotor will only turn
when the first rotor

00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:17.293
reaches a certain position.

00:06:17.293 --> 00:06:19.170
There's a notch

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.589
that's connected to the ring.

00:06:21.589 --> 00:06:25.510
That notch will allow the lever
at the back to drop.

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:30.181
And then connect with the ratchet
on the next rotor along.

00:06:30.181 --> 00:06:35.228
So this rotor turns until the notch
comes into the turnover position.

00:06:35.228 --> 00:06:38.272
When it does
so that allows the second lever

00:06:38.272 --> 00:06:42.985
to drop in and engage with
the ratchet on the second rotor,

00:06:42.985 --> 00:06:45.405
which will then
allow the second rotor to turn.

00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:49.409
You'll see as the rotors turn

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:52.370
the second
rotor turned at the same time.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:53.079
So after

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:56.999
26 rotations of the first rotor,
the second one turns once.
- Yes.

00:06:56.999 --> 00:06:59.460
And then after 26 rotations
of the second rotor,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.130
the third one
turns once?
- Yes. Absolutely.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.966
Yes. So the third one turns
very very rarely.

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:06.134
Yes. Okay.

00:07:06.134 --> 00:07:08.136
The operator can slot
the three rotors

00:07:08.136 --> 00:07:09.846
into the machine in any order.

00:07:09.846 --> 00:07:14.267
That's three times two times one
equals six possible permutations.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:15.935
Then there's the starting position

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:18.062
of each rotor,
which is shown in the window

00:07:18.062 --> 00:07:19.772
before the operator starts typing.

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.649
This is called the window setting.

00:07:21.649 --> 00:07:26.446
There are 26 numbers on each rotor,
so that's 26 cubed possibilities.

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:28.698
Multiply that by six possible
rotor orders.

00:07:28.698 --> 00:07:29.490
And that's over

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:33.453
100,000 ways to set up
this commercial Enigma machine.

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:36.914
But what the Germans did was
they not only bought them,

00:07:36.914 --> 00:07:39.750
but they said we're going to modify
them.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.377
We're going to modify them in a way

00:07:41.377 --> 00:07:43.421
that nobody else knows
what we've done.

00:07:43.421 --> 00:07:45.423
There were a few ways
that they did this.

00:07:45.423 --> 00:07:49.135
So if I undo this clasp here,

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:51.220
I can actually turn the ring round.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:55.308
And that changes the relationship
between the letter that appears

00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:59.604
at the top
and the fixed wiring of the rotor.

00:07:59.604 --> 00:08:01.772
I see.

00:08:01.772 --> 00:08:04.192
So rotating the ring
does two things.

00:08:04.192 --> 00:08:06.569
First, it offsets
the letters from the rotors

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:08.237
internal wiring for example

00:08:08.237 --> 00:08:10.239
at first
this rotor connects 1 to 4.

00:08:10.239 --> 00:08:11.699
So it changes A to D.

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:13.409
But rotate the ring by one.

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:15.870
And that same wire
now connects 2 to 5.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.456
So it changes B to E instead.

00:08:18.456 --> 00:08:22.293
And second it changes the position
where turnover occurs on

00:08:22.293 --> 00:08:23.586
the adjacent ring.

00:08:23.586 --> 00:08:27.632
But there was another vastly
more complex upgrade.

00:08:27.632 --> 00:08:29.634
There is another part
of the machine

00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:32.887
that we haven't looked at yet,
and this was a part

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:36.682
of the machine that was added
by the German military.

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:38.976
It's called the plug board.

00:08:38.976 --> 00:08:43.105
Each of the keys is connected
by a wire to the plug board.

00:08:43.105 --> 00:08:44.106
So the plug board

00:08:44.106 --> 00:08:48.569
has a letter for each of the keys,
because it means that you can

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:52.365
substitute the letter,
say the first one we have here,

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:55.785
which is Q and that's plugged

00:08:55.785 --> 00:08:58.788
into R.

00:08:58.788 --> 00:09:03.834
The Q coming from the keyboard
will go into the entry wheel

00:09:03.834 --> 00:09:06.879
into the first rotor as an R,

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:10.591
but if there's nothing plugged in
it will carry straight through.

00:09:10.591 --> 00:09:12.468
Now let's consider how this impacts

00:09:12.468 --> 00:09:14.679
the number of ways
messages can be encrypted.

00:09:14.679 --> 00:09:17.306
This is also known as the key
space.

00:09:17.306 --> 00:09:19.308
The revolvable rings
change the position

00:09:19.308 --> 00:09:21.477
where the second
two rings would turn over.

00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:24.397
So that's 26 squared possibilities.

00:09:24.397 --> 00:09:27.441
And then on the plug board,
if you swapped just one

00:09:27.441 --> 00:09:31.988
pair of letters, that would
introduce 325 more possibilities.

00:09:31.988 --> 00:09:33.155
But in the late

00:09:33.155 --> 00:09:36.909
1930s, the Nazis typically swapped
around six letters.

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:40.162
And that's 100 billion
possibilities.

00:09:40.162 --> 00:09:43.583
Overall that brings the grand total
for the key space to over

00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:47.253
seven times ten to the power of 18.

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:50.464
But as long as the machine
is set up exactly how it was

00:09:50.464 --> 00:09:53.884
at the start of the encryption,
you can type in the ciphertext

00:09:53.884 --> 00:09:56.637
and get out
the original message intact.

00:09:56.637 --> 00:09:59.348
This is also called the plain text.

00:09:59.348 --> 00:10:03.060
If both machines are in the correct
settings and I press an E,

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.606
it will encode to a k going through
the complicated set of wires.

00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:10.359
But if I press the K under the same
settings, it'll go back to an E.

00:10:10.359 --> 00:10:13.571
So it's like a closed loop of
electricity.

00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:14.697
So we have a k.

00:10:16.574 --> 00:10:21.370
Oh, it seems to be working! E.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:24.540
The way the Germans ensured
that all enigmas on a particular

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.835
network were set up in
the same way was by sending out

00:10:27.835 --> 00:10:31.005
prearranged instructions
for each day of the month.

00:10:31.005 --> 00:10:32.840
These were called the key sheets.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:34.091
So each morning

00:10:34.091 --> 00:10:36.385
when the operator
sent their first message,

00:10:36.385 --> 00:10:38.262
they would reconfigure
their machine

00:10:38.262 --> 00:10:41.223
to the new daily settings,
and that would allow them to

00:10:41.223 --> 00:10:45.227
encrypt and decrypt messages
to other Enigma operators.

00:10:45.227 --> 00:10:48.648
But what if you didn't
have the key sheets?

00:10:48.648 --> 00:10:51.901
Well, then you would have
to figure it out from scratch.

00:10:52.818 --> 00:10:54.612
By the end of the 1930s

00:10:54.612 --> 00:10:56.405
this problem had become urgent.

00:10:56.405 --> 00:10:59.909
As Nazi Germany grew more powerful,
British intelligence could sense

00:10:59.909 --> 00:11:03.037
that war was becoming
increasingly likely, and they knew

00:11:03.037 --> 00:11:06.290
that Enigma messages could reveal
what German forces were planning,

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:09.293
but only if the messages
could be deciphered in time.

00:11:09.293 --> 00:11:13.005
So British intelligence
assembled a secret workforce

00:11:13.005 --> 00:11:16.008
focused on doing just that.

00:11:16.008 --> 00:11:17.551
We're at Bletchley Park.

00:11:17.551 --> 00:11:21.305
This was Britain's most important
codebreaking operation

00:11:21.305 --> 00:11:22.765
during World War Two.

00:11:22.765 --> 00:11:26.268
Bletchley was an unremarkable town,
but it was situated conveniently

00:11:26.268 --> 00:11:30.189
between London and the country's
two most prestigious universities,

00:11:30.189 --> 00:11:32.149
Oxford and Cambridge,
which is where the

00:11:32.149 --> 00:11:34.235
British intelligence
sought their new recruits.

00:11:34.235 --> 00:11:36.737
And alongside
these academics, Bletchley Park

00:11:36.737 --> 00:11:40.032
also recruited women from the Royal
Navy known as Wrens,

00:11:40.032 --> 00:11:43.911
and the whole operation
was kept as secret as possible.

00:11:43.911 --> 00:11:47.957
To give you an idea of the secrecy,
here is something

00:11:47.957 --> 00:11:50.459
that the code breakers at Bletchley
Park had to follow.

00:11:50.459 --> 00:11:54.839
Some of the rules: do not talk at
meals or in transport or traveling.

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:56.716
Do not talk by your own fireside.

00:11:56.716 --> 00:11:58.926
Be careful even in your own hut.

00:11:58.926 --> 00:12:01.804
All in all, seems like a very
secretive place to work.

00:12:01.804 --> 00:12:07.601
Together this group of around 150 people
started trying to break the enigma.

00:12:07.601 --> 00:12:09.311
And here was their plan.

00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:11.814
First listening stations
would intercept German

00:12:11.814 --> 00:12:14.316
radio messages
and send them to Bletchley Park.

00:12:14.316 --> 00:12:17.528
Second, codebreakers
would analyze the day's traffic,

00:12:17.528 --> 00:12:20.573
looking for patterns and weaknesses
in the encryption.

00:12:20.573 --> 00:12:21.532
And third,

00:12:21.532 --> 00:12:24.618
they would use those clues
to try and guess the settings,

00:12:24.618 --> 00:12:26.120
and then they would
put those settings

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:29.331
into their own enigmas,
finally cracking the messages.

00:12:29.331 --> 00:12:32.084
But all this rested on
one key component

00:12:32.084 --> 00:12:35.379
the British needed a working
version of the Nazi Enigma machine

00:12:35.379 --> 00:12:38.424
to figure out the settings,
but they didn't have one.

00:12:38.424 --> 00:12:42.178
See, on top of adding
revolving rings and the plug board.

00:12:42.178 --> 00:12:44.263
The Nazis had made another upgrade.

00:12:44.263 --> 00:12:47.391
They had also changed the wiring
inside each rotor.

00:12:47.391 --> 00:12:49.769
That meant the Nazi rotors
scrambled the letters

00:12:49.769 --> 00:12:52.480
in a completely different way
from the commercial Enigma,

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:53.439
and without knowing

00:12:53.439 --> 00:12:56.108
the wiring of the Nazi rotors,
it was impossible

00:12:56.108 --> 00:12:59.111
to determine the settings
the Nazis were using each day.

00:12:59.111 --> 00:13:00.946
So the codebreakers were stuck.

00:13:00.946 --> 00:13:02.698
So it was completely hopeless.

00:13:02.698 --> 00:13:07.661
And that was the situation
at the beginning of July 1939.

00:13:07.661 --> 00:13:09.705
They really hadn't
made any progress at all.

00:13:09.705 --> 00:13:12.333
But what the British
and the Germans didn't know

00:13:12.333 --> 00:13:14.543
was that years earlier,
another country

00:13:14.543 --> 00:13:17.797
had already launched
a secret effort to break Enigma.

00:13:18.672 --> 00:13:21.550
In 1931,
an employee at the German Armies

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:25.179
cipher office made contact
with French intelligence, offering

00:13:25.179 --> 00:13:28.849
to sell secrets about the Enigma
in exchange for a handsome fee.

00:13:28.849 --> 00:13:30.893
He handed over
operating procedures,

00:13:30.893 --> 00:13:33.062
sample messages,
and even key sheets.

00:13:33.062 --> 00:13:35.189
Now, the French
didn't know what to do with them,

00:13:35.189 --> 00:13:37.483
so they shared them
with their Polish allies,

00:13:37.483 --> 00:13:40.027
and the Polish gave the sheets
to their codebreaking

00:13:40.027 --> 00:13:44.031
team of mathematicians,
who spotted a vulnerability.

00:13:44.031 --> 00:13:46.367
After an officer set up his enigma
for the day.

00:13:46.367 --> 00:13:47.910
He had to choose three

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:52.039
random letters before
sending a message, for example GEX.

00:13:52.039 --> 00:13:55.709
Then he would transmit these three
letters, encrypted on the day's

00:13:55.709 --> 00:13:57.920
default settings,
and he'd do it twice,

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.338
just in case of a bad signal.

00:13:59.338 --> 00:14:03.717
For example, the encrypted letters
would come out as ASDEIW.

00:14:03.717 --> 00:14:07.388
After that, he would move
his rotors such that these same

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:11.267
three letters GEX
would appear in his window up here.

00:14:11.267 --> 00:14:13.310
These were the so-called window
settings.

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:17.106
And finally he would be able to
type out the rest of the message.

00:14:17.106 --> 00:14:19.692
Now the receiver would decipher
the first six

00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:23.487
letters of the message,
and they would get GEX GEX

00:14:23.487 --> 00:14:26.866
and they would know that they had
to change their window settings

00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:29.869
to GEX at this point, and only then

00:14:29.869 --> 00:14:32.913
would they be able to decipher
the rest of the message.

00:14:32.913 --> 00:14:35.165
So it was encrypted twice.
- Right.

00:14:35.165 --> 00:14:38.586
Which was a security flaw,
because if you encrypt something

00:14:38.586 --> 00:14:41.797
twice,
you have a relationship created

00:14:41.797 --> 00:14:46.176
by the first letter of the key
that's been chosen.

00:14:46.176 --> 00:14:47.887
And the fourth letter,

00:14:47.887 --> 00:14:50.764
the same letter has been encrypted
because you're sending it twice.

00:14:50.764 --> 00:14:53.642
Okay. Just the three second letter
and the fifth letter as well.

00:14:53.642 --> 00:14:55.102
Okay I see.

00:14:55.102 --> 00:14:57.479
And they had this brilliant guy
called Marian Rejewski

00:14:57.479 --> 00:14:59.231
who essentially said, oh,

00:14:59.231 --> 00:15:02.109
I recognize this is a problem
in permutation theory

00:15:02.109 --> 00:15:04.737
from when I was doing
my undergraduate mathematics.

00:15:04.737 --> 00:15:10.451
This meant that by 1933,
the Poles had reconstructed

00:15:10.451 --> 00:15:15.080
the wiring
of the military version of Enigma,

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.125
even though they'd never seen one,
they never captured one.

00:15:18.125 --> 00:15:20.419
And so that meant
they were able to reverse engineer

00:15:20.419 --> 00:15:23.505
and make replica Enigma machines,
and then they could

00:15:23.505 --> 00:15:24.340
start working out

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:28.761
what the settings problem was
and how to how to solve that.

00:15:28.761 --> 00:15:34.058
So by the mid 1930s,
the Poles were actually able

00:15:34.058 --> 00:15:37.144
to read German military
Enigma traffic.

00:15:37.144 --> 00:15:39.897
But in 1939 the Nazis
were getting ready to invade.

00:15:39.897 --> 00:15:44.568
So they upped their security again
by first making two extra rotors.

00:15:44.568 --> 00:15:46.987
So now it was five to choose from,
not three.

00:15:46.987 --> 00:15:48.989
And that's five times
four times three.

00:15:48.989 --> 00:15:51.075
So 60 possible orders

00:15:51.075 --> 00:15:54.244
that increased the key space
by a factor of ten.

00:15:54.244 --> 00:15:56.372
And on top of that
they switched from using six

00:15:56.372 --> 00:15:58.040
plug board swaps to ten.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:01.418
That's around 150 trillion
combinations from the plug

00:16:01.418 --> 00:16:02.670
board alone.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:05.923
So all in all, the key space
had grown by a factor of 15,000

00:16:05.923 --> 00:16:09.051
to over 100 sextillion.

00:16:09.051 --> 00:16:12.262
The Polish needed help,
and by July 1939,

00:16:12.262 --> 00:16:14.306
they knew a German
invasion was likely.

00:16:14.306 --> 00:16:16.308
So the Poles called
an urgent meeting

00:16:16.308 --> 00:16:18.435
with British
and French intelligence.

00:16:18.435 --> 00:16:20.104
They revealed everything they knew
about

00:16:20.104 --> 00:16:24.233
the Enigma machine itself,
the wiring, and they revealed

00:16:24.233 --> 00:16:27.820
everything they knew about
the daily setup of the machine.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.489
Just over a month
later, Poland was invaded.

00:16:30.489 --> 00:16:33.450
Their codebreakers fled and
they had to leave behind the device

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:37.538
they'd invented to break into
Enigma messages called The Bomba.

00:16:37.538 --> 00:16:39.164
The Brits were now on their own.

00:16:39.164 --> 00:16:41.834
They were armed with the knowledge
given to them by the poles,

00:16:41.834 --> 00:16:44.878
but now it was up
to them to break the enigma.

00:16:45.879 --> 00:16:48.674
Our producer
has crafted an Enigma message,

00:16:48.674 --> 00:16:51.176
and I'm going to try and use
all of the tips and tricks

00:16:51.176 --> 00:16:53.846
that the British knew about
to try and crack this thing,

00:16:53.846 --> 00:16:56.348
see if I can get it right.
FAO Gregor.

00:16:56.348 --> 00:16:59.309
Urgent decryption required.

00:16:59.309 --> 00:17:00.185
We're on it.

00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:04.314
Okay, so the first thing to look at
is the top of the message.

00:17:04.314 --> 00:17:08.027
By the 1940s, the Germans quickly
realized that encrypting

00:17:08.027 --> 00:17:08.861
three letters

00:17:08.861 --> 00:17:12.072
twice at the start of each message
was a terrible security flaw.

00:17:12.072 --> 00:17:13.907
So they came up with a new system.

00:17:13.907 --> 00:17:15.117
After setting up the Enigma,

00:17:15.117 --> 00:17:16.785
the operator would now start each

00:17:16.785 --> 00:17:20.706
message with three random letters,
for example VER,

00:17:20.706 --> 00:17:23.417
and they would send them
completely unencrypted,

00:17:23.417 --> 00:17:25.586
naked over the air,
and then they would set

00:17:25.586 --> 00:17:27.713
their own window
settings to those three letters.

00:17:27.713 --> 00:17:29.048
So VER.

00:17:29.048 --> 00:17:32.634
With that the operator would pick
another three random letters,

00:17:32.634 --> 00:17:33.635
say ITA.

00:17:33.635 --> 00:17:36.805
But this time they would type them
through the Enigma.

00:17:36.805 --> 00:17:40.559
These three letters were called
the indicator, and after this

00:17:40.559 --> 00:17:42.061
they would set their own window

00:17:42.061 --> 00:17:45.397
settings to the indicator
so it reads ITA before

00:17:45.397 --> 00:17:48.734
finally typing
through their actual message.

00:17:48.734 --> 00:17:50.986
Likewise,
when a receiver got the message,

00:17:50.986 --> 00:17:52.738
they would know that
the first three letters

00:17:52.738 --> 00:17:54.531
VER came through unencrypted

00:17:54.531 --> 00:17:56.241
and they would put them
into their window settings

00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:58.619
and type out the next three letters
in the message.

00:17:58.619 --> 00:17:59.453
They would get three

00:17:59.453 --> 00:18:02.664
decrypted letters out, ITA,
and they would put those

00:18:02.664 --> 00:18:04.124
into their window settings next.

00:18:04.124 --> 00:18:07.586
And now they were finally ready
to read the rest of the message.

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:10.923
So a receiver is only able to

00:18:10.923 --> 00:18:11.840
decrypt the message

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:14.885
if they could follow these
two randomized checkpoints

00:18:14.885 --> 00:18:17.137
at the start of each message,
which should have been

00:18:17.137 --> 00:18:18.472
a great security addition,

00:18:18.472 --> 00:18:21.475
except people are terrible
at being random.

00:18:21.475 --> 00:18:25.104
People
very rarely choose random letters.

00:18:25.104 --> 00:18:29.233
So, for instance,
the German operator's

00:18:29.233 --> 00:18:32.986
girlfriend's name was one
that was chosen quite a lot.

00:18:32.986 --> 00:18:36.365
So for instance,
one common one is Cilla.

00:18:36.365 --> 00:18:38.659
Okay.
- Which would be CIL.

00:18:38.659 --> 00:18:41.870
CIL.
- Hence the term cillies.

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:44.123
And it wasn't just girlfriends
names either.

00:18:44.123 --> 00:18:48.710
So if the window setting you
send in plaintext is BER,

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:52.339
like you might be like, well,
I wonder if they just chose

00:18:52.339 --> 00:18:53.257
Berlin.

00:18:53.257 --> 00:18:55.050
And so you would deduce,
you just say

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:58.762
I guess the indicator
is probably LIN.

00:18:58.762 --> 00:19:02.099
Okay.
So my message starts with Lon.

00:19:02.099 --> 00:19:05.769
So I'm guessing that the operator
just chose London.

00:19:05.769 --> 00:19:09.106
So I know that CNG were encrypted

00:19:09.106 --> 00:19:12.442
from DON.

00:19:12.442 --> 00:19:15.737
Okay, so here's a universal
simulator for the Enigma.

00:19:15.737 --> 00:19:16.697
And I can actually show you

00:19:16.697 --> 00:19:20.534
that if I put in the window
settings on the rotors.

00:19:20.534 --> 00:19:24.788
LON, I know that if I type CNG,

00:19:24.788 --> 00:19:28.667
I'm supposed to get out DON
for London, but for this to work,

00:19:28.667 --> 00:19:32.462
I need to guess the correct rotor
order out of all of the rotors,

00:19:32.462 --> 00:19:35.465
and I need to get the correct
ring settings and the plug board.

00:19:35.465 --> 00:19:37.384
So there's still a lot of guesswork
to do

00:19:37.384 --> 00:19:39.511
for me
to be able to get this right.

00:19:39.511 --> 00:19:43.849
Okay, so I'm now going to record
some me actually trying this

00:19:43.849 --> 00:19:48.187
DON no, DON no.

00:19:49.855 --> 00:19:51.481
I'm, I'm struggling a bit.

00:19:51.481 --> 00:19:54.026
I won't lie, I am struggling a bit.

00:19:54.026 --> 00:19:55.110
But there was another way

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:57.154
the laziness of the operators
came through.

00:19:57.154 --> 00:20:00.282
It was spotted by a British
codebreaker, John Herivel.

00:20:00.866 --> 00:20:03.076
He compared the messages
from all over the network

00:20:03.076 --> 00:20:03.535
that came

00:20:03.535 --> 00:20:07.289
from many different operators, and
he noticed something interesting.

00:20:07.289 --> 00:20:08.415
Those three letters

00:20:08.415 --> 00:20:11.627
the operators started their
messages with appeared random,

00:20:11.627 --> 00:20:15.047
but they all seemed to be
a very similar random.

00:20:15.047 --> 00:20:18.592
If you looked at just the first
letter every operator chose across

00:20:18.592 --> 00:20:19.718
hundreds of messages.

00:20:19.718 --> 00:20:22.346
They weren't spread
evenly across the alphabet.

00:20:22.346 --> 00:20:25.265
They centered around
a specific location.

00:20:25.265 --> 00:20:27.643
Same for the second letter
and same for the third.

00:20:27.643 --> 00:20:29.895
This happened
because the operating procedure

00:20:29.895 --> 00:20:32.981
required the operators to shuffle
the rotors from the default

00:20:32.981 --> 00:20:35.776
key sheet settings for the day,
those ring settings.

00:20:35.776 --> 00:20:38.987
And as it turned out,
a significant number of operators

00:20:38.987 --> 00:20:41.990
only shuffled their rotors
by a position or two.

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:42.699
It's sort of

00:20:42.699 --> 00:20:45.452
like how people lock their bikes
and only move the rotors

00:20:45.452 --> 00:20:46.662
a small amount.

00:20:46.662 --> 00:20:47.913
Following the same logic,

00:20:47.913 --> 00:20:48.747
Harrisville concluded

00:20:48.747 --> 00:20:50.749
that the clustering of window
settings around

00:20:50.749 --> 00:20:53.001
a particular set of three letters
throughout the day

00:20:53.001 --> 00:20:55.879
actually revealed
what the daily ring settings were,

00:20:55.879 --> 00:20:58.840
so this became known
as the Herivel tip.

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:01.802
So I know that the Herivel tip or the ring
settings are MON,

00:21:01.802 --> 00:21:03.262
so I can convert them into,

00:21:03.262 --> 00:21:05.222
well, what
that letter is in the alphabet.

00:21:05.222 --> 00:21:09.184
So M is the 13th, 15th is O,
and then 14th is N.

00:21:09.184 --> 00:21:11.353
I don't know this by heart,
I googled it.

00:21:11.353 --> 00:21:15.524
Now I know that if I type DON,
I'm supposed to be getting CNG out.

00:21:15.524 --> 00:21:19.695
So now I've got to guess
the rotor order for what rotors

00:21:19.695 --> 00:21:20.946
they were using today.

00:21:20.946 --> 00:21:21.989
And there were some hints,

00:21:21.989 --> 00:21:26.994
but besides that, it was basically
just a lot of brute force.

00:21:26.994 --> 00:21:28.036
You just had to try them.

00:21:28.036 --> 00:21:30.622
Let's try one, two three. Don.

00:21:30.622 --> 00:21:33.709
No, no, not even close.

00:21:34.418 --> 00:21:37.838
132 15 and 14

00:21:38.839 --> 00:21:41.008
ZNG. Oh there we go.

00:21:41.008 --> 00:21:45.012
ZNG sounds pretty close to CNG
that we're supposed to get.

00:21:45.012 --> 00:21:48.390
I think I've guessed the rotor
order to be one, three and two.

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:50.642
All that remains
now is the plug board.

00:21:50.642 --> 00:21:54.604
So maybe a Z needs to become a C

00:21:54.604 --> 00:21:56.898
a Z and a C.

00:21:56.898 --> 00:21:57.774
DON.

00:21:57.774 --> 00:21:58.358
CNG!

00:21:58.358 --> 00:21:59.026
There we go.

00:21:59.026 --> 00:22:02.863
So if I was to change my window
settings now to Don, that's

00:22:02.863 --> 00:22:05.866
that second step of randomization.

00:22:05.866 --> 00:22:10.078
...THPKAKTQOG...

00:22:10.078 --> 00:22:11.663
okay this kind of looks like text.

00:22:11.663 --> 00:22:13.373
Looks like James.

00:22:13.373 --> 00:22:15.584
A D and an E.

00:22:15.584 --> 00:22:19.379
Haha, okay!

00:22:19.379 --> 00:22:21.173
Oh that took a while.

00:22:21.173 --> 00:22:22.591
There was a lot of plug board
guesses,

00:22:22.591 --> 00:22:24.634
but I think
I finally figured it out.

00:22:24.634 --> 00:22:26.303
It says James is a legend.

00:22:26.303 --> 00:22:27.888
James the producer.

00:22:27.888 --> 00:22:30.182
Okay, we got it. There
we go. It took.

00:22:30.182 --> 00:22:31.475
How long did it take me?

00:22:31.475 --> 00:22:32.768
Pencils down, everyone.

00:22:32.768 --> 00:22:34.561
One hour straight.

00:22:34.561 --> 00:22:35.604
That's tough.

00:22:35.604 --> 00:22:38.106
But that was actually an easy
example.

00:22:38.106 --> 00:22:39.816
Bletchley codebreakers
would have been faced

00:22:39.816 --> 00:22:40.484
with figuring out

00:22:40.484 --> 00:22:43.195
multiple plug board swaps,
and in a single message,

00:22:43.195 --> 00:22:45.530
it would be highly unlikely to use
both the Herivel

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:48.825
tip and the cillies,
even with the operator errors

00:22:48.825 --> 00:22:51.161
to help them, codebreakers
at Bletchley had to trawl

00:22:51.161 --> 00:22:53.038
through hundreds of messages
each day

00:22:53.038 --> 00:22:55.582
just to get a clue of what
the Enigma settings were.

00:22:55.582 --> 00:22:58.627
And doing
this manually was a huge task.

00:22:58.627 --> 00:23:01.671
To make it even harder,
some networks were much more secure

00:23:01.671 --> 00:23:02.172
and didn't

00:23:02.172 --> 00:23:06.093
have any of these human errors,
making them far tougher to crack.

00:23:06.093 --> 00:23:09.888
So what Bletchley Park needed
was an automated way to break these

00:23:09.888 --> 00:23:11.181
Enigma messages,

00:23:11.181 --> 00:23:15.310
and that came from exploiting flaws
in the machine itself.

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:18.188
You see, the weakest part
of any encryption device

00:23:18.188 --> 00:23:19.564
is not the device itself.

00:23:19.564 --> 00:23:22.442
It's the human that's operating it.
And kind of like the Enigma.

00:23:22.442 --> 00:23:23.110
Human error

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:26.488
is also one of the biggest reasons
why our information leaks

00:23:26.488 --> 00:23:27.364
onto the internet.

