00:24:34.014 --> 00:24:37.017
So I want to thank incognito for
sponsoring this part of the video.

00:24:37.017 --> 00:24:40.020
And now back
to breaking the Enigma.

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:41.271
So Bletchley Park

00:24:41.271 --> 00:24:44.399
needed to develop a way
to automate the breaking of Enigma.

00:24:44.399 --> 00:24:47.360
And luckily for them,
there was one man in particular

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:50.197
who was uniquely qualified
for the job.

00:24:50.197 --> 00:24:52.240
Alan Turing had already gained fame

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:52.908
for his paper

00:24:52.908 --> 00:24:56.119
on a device that since became known
as a Turing machine,

00:24:56.119 --> 00:24:59.706
so the British were quick
to recruit him at Bletchley Park.

00:24:59.706 --> 00:25:01.041
When Turing arrived,

00:25:01.041 --> 00:25:04.252
he realized that if he could
describe the process of breaking

00:25:04.252 --> 00:25:08.006
the Enigma as a sequence of some
logical tests, then he could

00:25:08.006 --> 00:25:11.343
theoretically devise a machine
that would do the job for him.

00:25:11.343 --> 00:25:15.430
So we're in hut eight,
and this room is the one

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:18.600
that apparently Alan Turing
used as his office when he was

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:22.062
put in charge of the naval
Enigma problem.

00:25:22.062 --> 00:25:25.482
They've changed a tin

00:25:25.482 --> 00:25:30.820
tea mug to the radiator,
which Alan Turing allegedly did.

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:34.032
I think what happened was that
people would take his mug away

00:25:34.032 --> 00:25:38.370
to wash it up, and then he couldn't
find it when he wanted it.

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:41.373
Can you tell me more about, like,
what kind of a person he was?

00:25:41.373 --> 00:25:44.084
Really quite wicked sense of humor.

00:25:44.084 --> 00:25:47.796
Very irreverent
and very interesting as well to

00:25:47.796 --> 00:25:50.298
to talk to if you were interested
in the right kind of thing.

00:25:50.298 --> 00:25:54.094
So if you want to talk to him about
a football game, then frankly,

00:25:54.094 --> 00:25:54.886
think again.

00:25:54.886 --> 00:25:56.137
But if you want to talk to him

00:25:56.137 --> 00:25:58.515
about the possibility
to build a machine

00:25:58.515 --> 00:25:59.349
that might be able to play

00:25:59.349 --> 00:26:03.186
a game of chess,
he’d have lots to say about that.

00:26:03.186 --> 00:26:05.272
To automatically break
the enigma, Turing

00:26:05.272 --> 00:26:08.066
needed a logical test
that a machine could perform

00:26:08.066 --> 00:26:11.152
some series of procedures,
after which the machine would say,

00:26:11.152 --> 00:26:13.363
oh no, these are not the Enigma
settings. They're wrong.

00:26:13.363 --> 00:26:14.656
You should try something else.

00:26:14.656 --> 00:26:17.826
Or yes, these could be the correct
Enigma settings.

00:26:17.826 --> 00:26:20.203
Now you should try and decipher
the message with them.

00:26:20.203 --> 00:26:22.080
The test that he ended up
devising came

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:25.834
from the fundamental flaw
in the Enigma, and surprisingly, it

00:26:25.834 --> 00:26:27.544
exploited some of the most

00:26:27.544 --> 00:26:30.880
unimportant and mundane messages
that the Nazis were sending.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:33.633
Certain German outposts
sent very similar messages

00:26:33.633 --> 00:26:36.678
at the same time every day,
and that would be a weather report.

00:26:36.678 --> 00:26:38.638
So, for example, if Bletchley Park

00:26:38.638 --> 00:26:40.932
intercepted a message
every day at 6 a.m.

00:26:40.932 --> 00:26:44.436
from an operator in Biscay,
the code breaker might reasonably

00:26:44.436 --> 00:26:47.480
guess that somewhere inside
this message, there would be text

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:52.193
that says weather report Biscay,
or in German, WETTERVORGERSAGEBISKAYA.

00:26:52.193 --> 00:26:55.322
And there was a way to guess
where that text was encrypted

00:26:55.322 --> 00:26:56.489
in the message.

00:26:56.489 --> 00:27:00.869
If I'm pressing an L,
I can keep pressing the L on here,

00:27:00.869 --> 00:27:05.915
and the L on the lamp
panel will never light up.

00:27:05.915 --> 00:27:08.668
You can't encrypt a key
to become itself.

00:27:08.668 --> 00:27:14.507
No. Because the current can't
flow back through the same wire,

00:27:14.507 --> 00:27:16.885
it has to go
through a different wire.

00:27:16.885 --> 00:27:20.055
That means I can take this word
and I can shift it over the

00:27:20.055 --> 00:27:22.474
enciphered text
until I find a position

00:27:22.474 --> 00:27:24.476
where no letter is
enciphered as itself.

00:27:24.476 --> 00:27:28.146
So if I start, for example,
with W and Q aligned

00:27:28.146 --> 00:27:30.106
so that the two messages
align at the start

00:27:30.106 --> 00:27:34.611
and I scanned through here,
this S is enciphered as itself.

00:27:34.611 --> 00:27:36.154
So that isn't a possible solution.

00:27:36.154 --> 00:27:39.157
So I'm going to shift it
one letter down and try again.

00:27:39.157 --> 00:27:42.619
Now I have a V enciphered as a V
and an E enciphered as an E.

00:27:42.619 --> 00:27:43.328
So this doesn't work.

00:27:43.328 --> 00:27:45.163
So I'm going to shift
another position.

00:27:45.163 --> 00:27:47.165
Oh no I have an R enciphered
as an R.

00:27:47.165 --> 00:27:49.376
So that doesn't work either
I'm going to shift it

00:27:49.376 --> 00:27:50.669
one more.

00:27:50.669 --> 00:27:51.503
That all looks good.

00:27:51.503 --> 00:27:53.505
So here we go. Bingo.

00:27:53.505 --> 00:27:55.924
Now there's no letter
that is enciphered as itself.

00:27:55.924 --> 00:27:56.841
So my guess

00:27:56.841 --> 00:28:00.804
is that this part of the enciphered
text says weather report Biscay.

00:28:00.804 --> 00:28:03.848
And this guess at what the
enciphered text actually represents

00:28:03.848 --> 00:28:06.101
is called a crib.

00:28:06.101 --> 00:28:09.729
At Bletchley Park in hut eight they had
what was called the cribbing room.

00:28:09.729 --> 00:28:11.147
This is Jonah Weinbaum.

00:28:11.147 --> 00:28:12.482
He wrote his thesis on

00:28:12.482 --> 00:28:14.025
how the enigma was broken.

00:28:14.025 --> 00:28:19.864
And the cribbing room, their job was to identify
good, strong,

00:28:19.864 --> 00:28:23.159
consistent cribs,
which consisted of either

00:28:23.159 --> 00:28:27.247
weather messages, patterns
they had noticed across messages.

00:28:27.247 --> 00:28:30.709
The usage of slang
 or shorthand.

00:28:30.709 --> 00:28:31.501
After Turing

00:28:31.501 --> 00:28:33.962
was sure that he had a crib
that reasonably guessed

00:28:33.962 --> 00:28:35.714
what the encrypted message
stood for

00:28:35.714 --> 00:28:38.466
he started with his logical test.

00:28:38.466 --> 00:28:40.468
He assumed that
at the start of each message,

00:28:40.468 --> 00:28:44.764
the window settings for the Enigma
were ZZZ or 26, 26, 26,

00:28:44.764 --> 00:28:48.476
and that the second rotor hadn't
turned in this part of the message.

00:28:48.476 --> 00:28:50.603
So as soon as the first letter
is typed,

00:28:50.603 --> 00:28:54.357
the rightmost rotor turns to A
or 1. At that position

00:28:54.357 --> 00:28:57.318
typing
a W in the machine outputs an R,

00:28:57.318 --> 00:28:59.320
and that means that
with the Enigma machine

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:04.117
in this particular state, W
is wired to R, and R is wired to W.

00:29:04.117 --> 00:29:07.036
As each letter is typed,
the rightmost rotor moves

00:29:07.036 --> 00:29:09.748
one step forward,
so when the window on the right

00:29:09.748 --> 00:29:10.790
reads two,

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:16.963
E and W are wired together,
at three, it's T and I, and so on.

00:29:16.963 --> 00:29:20.341
Now Turing notice that this
encryption produced a kind of loop.

00:29:20.341 --> 00:29:24.179
For example, at position six,
the Enigma machine maps R to Y,

00:29:24.179 --> 00:29:29.184
but at 22 it maps Y to S,
and at nine it maps R to S.

00:29:29.184 --> 00:29:30.143
So Turing

00:29:30.143 --> 00:29:31.436
imagined taking a clone

00:29:31.436 --> 00:29:34.022
of the Enigma machine
at each of these positions.

00:29:34.022 --> 00:29:37.025
Then the only difference between
these machines is just the position

00:29:37.025 --> 00:29:38.359
of that rightmost rotor.

00:29:38.359 --> 00:29:40.987
It's either 6, 9, or 22.

00:29:40.987 --> 00:29:44.199
Now look what happens if you wire
these three machines back to back,

00:29:44.199 --> 00:29:48.036
so that the output of Enigma
6 becomes the input of Enigma 22,

00:29:48.036 --> 00:29:51.206
and then the output of 22 becomes
the input of nine.

00:29:51.206 --> 00:29:52.582
If Turing hit the letter

00:29:52.582 --> 00:29:55.084
R on the first machine,
it would output a Y.

00:29:55.084 --> 00:29:57.253
Then the second machine
would then transform that

00:29:57.253 --> 00:29:59.380
Y into an S,
and the third machine would take

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:02.801
that S back to an R,
creating a perfect loop.

00:30:02.801 --> 00:30:04.761
R turns back into R.

00:30:04.761 --> 00:30:07.639
Now for this to happen,
the settings on these three

00:30:07.639 --> 00:30:09.557
Enigma machines have to match
the settings

00:30:09.557 --> 00:30:11.518
used to encrypt
the original message,

00:30:11.518 --> 00:30:13.686
but if the settings don't match,
for example,

00:30:13.686 --> 00:30:16.022
if the rotors used in
the enigmas are different,

00:30:16.022 --> 00:30:20.026
then the test is extremely unlikely
to produce a loop and R

00:30:20.026 --> 00:30:23.196
should turn into a different letter
instead.

00:30:23.196 --> 00:30:25.907
This gave Turing the logical test
he was after.

00:30:25.907 --> 00:30:27.784
Theoretically,
he could create a machine

00:30:27.784 --> 00:30:30.829
that looks through all possible
Enigma settings, such that

00:30:30.829 --> 00:30:34.415
when you offset the first rotor
by 6, 22, and 9 an R

00:30:34.415 --> 00:30:37.377
is transformed back into itself.

00:30:37.377 --> 00:30:39.587
Now, out of millions of possible
settings,

00:30:39.587 --> 00:30:40.839
there are multiple combinations

00:30:40.839 --> 00:30:43.174
that can transform
an R back into itself

00:30:43.174 --> 00:30:46.886
at positions 6, 22, and 9.
Turing's test could return

00:30:46.886 --> 00:30:50.807
hundreds of false positives, so
he needed more logical constraints,

00:30:50.807 --> 00:30:51.599
which came in the form

00:30:51.599 --> 00:30:54.686
of more loops from that crib
we saw earlier.

00:30:54.686 --> 00:30:55.353
Back in the message

00:30:55.353 --> 00:30:59.482
notice that R maps to W
at position 1 and then W to E

00:30:59.482 --> 00:31:02.610
right after that,
and then goes E to O at position

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:06.447
8, O to S, and then back to R,
which completes the loop.

00:31:06.447 --> 00:31:07.657
Following this process,

00:31:07.657 --> 00:31:11.786
Turing built up an entire menu of
these loops of logical constraints.

00:31:11.786 --> 00:31:14.330
But this whole test
rests on the assumption

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:16.749
that we've guessed the right
rotor order.

00:31:16.749 --> 00:31:19.711
Remember that the Germans
had five rotors to choose from,

00:31:19.711 --> 00:31:22.213
and they could slot
any three of them into the machine

00:31:22.213 --> 00:31:23.214
in any order.

00:31:23.214 --> 00:31:25.675
That's 60 possible configurations.

00:31:25.675 --> 00:31:27.886
So Turing's
machines had to run through

00:31:27.886 --> 00:31:31.890
this entire process again and again
for each rotor order.

00:31:31.890 --> 00:31:35.935
And that still left one component
unaccounted for, the plug board

00:31:35.935 --> 00:31:40.148
and the 150 trillion
possible ways to hook it up.

00:31:40.148 --> 00:31:43.401
But even though this number
is ridiculously large, Turing

00:31:43.401 --> 00:31:47.030
found a simple way to isolate
the plug board's effects.

00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:49.699
Remember how a letter flows
through the Enigma?

00:31:49.699 --> 00:31:51.826
First through the plug board,
then through the rotors,

00:31:51.826 --> 00:31:53.786
then back out
via the plug board again

00:31:53.786 --> 00:31:57.081
so we can break each enigma
into three steps.

00:31:57.081 --> 00:32:00.251
If you type an R into the Enigma,
it first gets transformed

00:32:00.251 --> 00:32:02.211
by the plug board
to, for example, a Z

00:32:02.211 --> 00:32:05.798
and then that gets transformed
by the rotors to a Q.

00:32:05.798 --> 00:32:09.427
Now this Q again gets transformed
by the plug board to an X.

00:32:09.427 --> 00:32:12.597
So X is the letter
that flows into the second enigma.

00:32:12.597 --> 00:32:15.850
But the plug board on the second
enigma is set up in the same way.

00:32:15.850 --> 00:32:18.061
So it swaps that X back to a Q.

00:32:18.061 --> 00:32:20.813
So Q goes to X goes to Q.

00:32:20.813 --> 00:32:24.233
In other words, back to back
plug boards cancel each other out.

00:32:24.233 --> 00:32:25.818
So we can actually simplify

00:32:25.818 --> 00:32:29.489
this long list of transformations
to this.

00:32:29.489 --> 00:32:32.492
That means Turing could treat
any series of Enigma machines

00:32:32.492 --> 00:32:34.285
as the plug board on the way in.

00:32:34.285 --> 00:32:36.496
Then the combined effect
of all the rotors

00:32:36.496 --> 00:32:38.873
and then the plug board
on the way out.

00:32:38.873 --> 00:32:41.751
But remember, with the correct
settings, these three

00:32:41.751 --> 00:32:45.338
Enigma machines
transformed an R back into an R,

00:32:45.338 --> 00:32:48.549
which means if the first plug board
transformed an R to a

00:32:48.549 --> 00:32:49.217
Z on the way

00:32:49.217 --> 00:32:52.762
in, then the last plug board
must have transformed a Z into an R

00:32:52.762 --> 00:32:53.972
on the way out.

00:32:53.972 --> 00:32:56.933
So the three rotors alone
must also create a loop,

00:32:56.933 --> 00:32:58.518
transforming that same letter

00:32:58.518 --> 00:33:02.563
Z back into itself
with no plug board needed at all.

00:33:02.563 --> 00:33:04.941
Much like the original loop
through the three enigmas,

00:33:04.941 --> 00:33:07.485
Turing realized
this formed the basis of a test,

00:33:07.485 --> 00:33:10.780
one that he could perform
using a simple electrical circuit.

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:11.906
He'd start with a guess,

00:33:11.906 --> 00:33:15.785
say that R is plugged to Z,
and set that wire live.

00:33:15.785 --> 00:33:18.788
If the guess was correct,
the three enigmas would transform

00:33:18.788 --> 00:33:22.792
that Z back to itself, leaving
a single loop of live wires.

00:33:22.792 --> 00:33:25.962
If the guest was wrong,
it's likely that Z would loop back

00:33:25.962 --> 00:33:28.548
to a different letter,
and that letter would loop round

00:33:28.548 --> 00:33:29.716
to another and another.

00:33:29.716 --> 00:33:30.383
So current

00:33:30.383 --> 00:33:32.427
would keep looping around
and around the circuit,

00:33:32.427 --> 00:33:34.470
setting multiple wires live.

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:37.557
But that would mean
that R was simultaneously plugged

00:33:37.557 --> 00:33:40.727
into multiple letters,
which is impossible.

00:33:40.727 --> 00:33:43.730
This contradiction allowed him
to rule out all these letters

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:45.732
as a candidate
for what R is connected

00:33:45.732 --> 00:33:48.985
to on the plug board,
and if all 26 wires went live,

00:33:48.985 --> 00:33:51.070
he knew that there was no possible
plug board

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:52.822
configurations
for that window setting,

00:33:52.822 --> 00:33:54.824
so his machine would move
the rotors

00:33:54.824 --> 00:33:56.951
one step forward
and start the test again.

00:33:56.951 --> 00:33:58.161
There were also cases

00:33:58.161 --> 00:34:01.914
where 25 wires could light
up, leaving just one dark.

00:34:01.914 --> 00:34:04.709
Since those 25 possibilities
had all been ruled out,

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:06.294
that wire that didn't light up

00:34:06.294 --> 00:34:08.463
pointed
to the only remaining candidate for

00:34:08.463 --> 00:34:10.506
are to be connected to on the plug
board.

00:34:10.506 --> 00:34:13.176
This was Turing's stroke of genius,

00:34:13.176 --> 00:34:16.804
even though there were 150 trillion
ways to configure the blackboard.

00:34:16.804 --> 00:34:18.931
Starting with this, one
guess would allow his machine

00:34:18.931 --> 00:34:22.268
to rule out multiple possibilities
or even reject incorrect

00:34:22.268 --> 00:34:23.519
settings entirely.

00:34:23.519 --> 00:34:26.481
The guests didn't even have to
be correct for this to work.

00:34:27.732 --> 00:34:28.816
They did do this by hand.

00:34:28.816 --> 00:34:30.693
They did these contradictions,

00:34:30.693 --> 00:34:34.113
you know, on a piece of paper,
following through what is implied.

00:34:34.113 --> 00:34:37.492
But Turing designed this machine

00:34:37.492 --> 00:34:41.370
so that it was instantaneous,
like it was at the speed,

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:42.163
not the speed of light.

00:34:42.163 --> 00:34:45.083
But the speed of electricity
flowing in a wire.

00:34:45.083 --> 00:34:46.250
With the help of an engineer,

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:49.629
Harold Keen, Turing
brought his device to life.

00:34:49.629 --> 00:34:53.883
Based on some of the ideas that they got
from the Polish Cipher Bureau

00:34:53.883 --> 00:34:57.762
just before the war
started, Turing and his colleagues

00:34:57.762 --> 00:34:59.013
came up with this.

00:35:04.519 --> 00:35:07.814
He called it The Bomb, named in
honor of the Polish

00:35:07.814 --> 00:35:11.109
Bomba, the device that the Polish
mathematicians invented to break

00:35:11.109 --> 00:35:13.152
the earlier versions of Enigma.

00:35:18.157 --> 00:35:19.283
First prototype,

00:35:19.283 --> 00:35:24.705
arrived in March of 1940,
which in itself is a pretty startling achievement,

00:35:24.705 --> 00:35:27.458
given the war only started
in September 1939.

00:35:27.458 --> 00:35:28.209
Yeah.

00:35:28.209 --> 00:35:32.338
To get the civil service,
to not only approve his design

00:35:32.338 --> 00:35:36.217
and then to get the finance
to be able to build the thing.

00:35:36.217 --> 00:35:37.468
Absolutely astonishing.

00:35:37.468 --> 00:35:41.931
Anyway, this is essentially 36
Enigma machines

00:35:41.931 --> 00:35:45.560
working in parallel,
working backwards.

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:48.646
So you start with an assumption
that you've made that

00:35:48.646 --> 00:35:54.360
a certain piece of ciphertext
represents certain plaintext meaning.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:56.863
Give all that to the machine,

00:35:56.863 --> 00:35:58.656
let the machine go to work.

00:35:58.656 --> 00:36:03.578
And at the end of the process,
the idea is you're going to derive

00:36:03.578 --> 00:36:07.373
the key of the day
that the original Enigma operator

00:36:07.373 --> 00:36:10.251
was using
to encipher that message,

00:36:10.251 --> 00:36:14.213
which means now you can read
every message that that guy sent,

00:36:14.213 --> 00:36:16.591
not just
the one that you're analyzing.

00:36:16.591 --> 00:36:18.050
And so when we run the machine,

00:36:18.050 --> 00:36:22.263
we're hoping for a condition
when every enigma will find

00:36:22.263 --> 00:36:25.349
the answer
it's been told to find all

00:36:25.349 --> 00:36:28.477
at the same exact instant.

00:36:28.477 --> 00:36:32.440
And when that happens, you've
essentially solve this equation

00:36:32.440 --> 00:36:34.984
and the machine will stop
and give you an answer.

00:36:34.984 --> 00:36:39.947
So what I'm going to do now is I'm
going to run the machine.
- Okay

00:36:39.947 --> 00:36:41.324
Let’s do it.

00:36:41.324 --> 00:36:42.825
It's going to be quite loud.

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:51.918
Eventually you're going to get

00:36:51.918 --> 00:36:58.049
through all the combinations
26 times 26 times 26

00:36:58.049 --> 00:37:00.760
17,500 or so.

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:02.511
Yeah,
it's going to take the machine

00:37:02.511 --> 00:37:07.016
about 13 minutes
to go through all of those.

00:37:07.016 --> 00:37:07.767
Right?

00:37:07.767 --> 00:37:08.851
And at some point,

00:37:08.851 --> 00:37:13.022
we're hoping that the machine is
going to find an answer that works.

00:37:13.022 --> 00:37:14.148
At which point

00:37:14.148 --> 00:37:15.816
it's going to come to a stop

00:37:15.816 --> 00:37:19.528
assuming the machine is actually
working correctly.

00:37:19.528 --> 00:37:21.822
What are the chances that it is
working correctly?

00:37:21.822 --> 00:37:23.115
About 90%.

00:37:23.115 --> 00:37:24.617
Okay, to be fair, that's good.

00:37:24.617 --> 00:37:27.453
It's better than my brain.
-And mine.

00:37:27.453 --> 00:37:29.872
Okay, so what's going to happen?

00:37:29.872 --> 00:37:31.582
Machines found a solution.

00:37:31.582 --> 00:37:32.667
Okay, there we go.

00:37:32.667 --> 00:37:34.043
And if you look at these drums

00:37:34.043 --> 00:37:38.256
on the right hand end, that's
where the solutions are indicated.

00:37:38.256 --> 00:37:40.049
These are called
the indicator drums.

00:37:40.049 --> 00:37:42.385
It's the output from the machine.

00:37:42.385 --> 00:37:45.471
And if you look very closely
you'll see a little pointer

00:37:45.471 --> 00:37:50.184
on each of those drums
pointing to the letters D, K and X.

00:37:50.184 --> 00:37:55.147
So that's the machine saying
I think DKX is an answer.

00:37:55.147 --> 00:37:58.234
Now I'm saying an answer,
not the answer.

00:37:58.234 --> 00:38:02.113
The problem is that you might
get multiple answers, because it's

00:38:02.113 --> 00:38:06.075
quite possible you'll find
several solutions to the equation.

00:38:06.075 --> 00:38:08.953
And there is a flaw hiding
in Turing's original test.

00:38:08.953 --> 00:38:12.164
Sometimes the machine would stop on
what looked like a clean, valid

00:38:12.164 --> 00:38:15.710
loop, but in reality, that loop
could be self-contradictory.

00:38:15.710 --> 00:38:18.546
It might imply, for example,
that R was plugged into both X

00:38:18.546 --> 00:38:21.841
and S on the plug board at once,
which is impossible.

00:38:21.841 --> 00:38:23.175
The machine had no way to

00:38:23.175 --> 00:38:25.761
catch this,
and every time the bomb stopped

00:38:25.761 --> 00:38:28.472
and offered a solution,
an operator would have to check it.

00:38:28.472 --> 00:38:30.141
So if there were 100 stops,

00:38:30.141 --> 00:38:32.476
each of which might have taken
15 minutes to check,

00:38:32.476 --> 00:38:35.604
that's more than 24 hours of work,
by which point

00:38:35.604 --> 00:38:38.024
the Enigma settings
would have already changed.

00:38:38.024 --> 00:38:39.734
The Bombe wasn't fast enough.

00:38:39.734 --> 00:38:42.153
By the time
the first Bombe was operational,

00:38:42.153 --> 00:38:45.406
the Nazis were already occupying
Austria and Czechoslovakia,

00:38:45.406 --> 00:38:47.158
and they had invaded Poland,

00:38:47.158 --> 00:38:49.785
killing hundreds of thousands
in the process.

00:38:49.785 --> 00:38:53.289
Bletchley Park desperately
needed a way to speed up The Bombe.

00:38:53.289 --> 00:38:56.709
Turing had managed expectations,
so it came as no great shock.

00:38:56.709 --> 00:38:57.668
After that

00:38:57.668 --> 00:39:01.547
a mathematician called Gordon
Welchman comes into the story.

00:39:01.547 --> 00:39:04.717
Welchman, another Cambridge
mathematician, comes

00:39:04.717 --> 00:39:08.179
to Bletchley Park,
working alongside Turing,

00:39:08.179 --> 00:39:11.932
and he comes up
with a brilliant modification.

00:39:11.932 --> 00:39:13.351
Welchman realized that guessing

00:39:13.351 --> 00:39:14.018
R is plugged to

00:39:14.018 --> 00:39:17.605
Y is exactly the same as guessing
that Y is plugged to R, so

00:39:17.605 --> 00:39:20.983
Welchman connected these two wires
such that if one went live,

00:39:20.983 --> 00:39:22.026
they both would.

00:39:22.026 --> 00:39:25.488
He would do the same for S
and Y and R and S,

00:39:25.488 --> 00:39:27.865
and this made it more likely
that a guess at the plug

00:39:27.865 --> 00:39:31.494
board would set multiple
wires live, so exactly the same

00:39:31.494 --> 00:39:34.580
input to the machine would rule out
more potential plug board swaps,

00:39:34.580 --> 00:39:38.209
and therefore drastically reduce
the number of false positives.

00:39:38.209 --> 00:39:40.753
Instead of the machine
stopping and giving you 100

00:39:40.753 --> 00:39:44.840
possible solutions to check out,
you only get four.

00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:48.219
Which means that process
was so much slicker.

00:39:48.219 --> 00:39:53.516
So welchman's improvement
made the thing practical.

00:39:53.516 --> 00:39:56.435
Welchman's modification
became known as the diagonal board,

00:39:56.435 --> 00:39:58.604
and Turing figured out
that in many cases,

00:39:58.604 --> 00:40:03.734
the diagonal board could eliminate
over 90% of false stops.

00:40:03.734 --> 00:40:05.820
This new
version of The Bombe was ready

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:09.657
in August 1940,
but by now France had fallen

00:40:09.657 --> 00:40:13.411
along with Norway and Denmark.
Britain was the last major power

00:40:13.411 --> 00:40:16.414
in Western Europe, and the Nazis
had begun an aerial onslaught

00:40:16.414 --> 00:40:19.083
trying to bring Britain
to its knees.

00:40:23.796 --> 00:40:27.466
We shall defend our island,
whatever the cost.

00:40:27.466 --> 00:40:30.052
We shall fight on beaches.

00:40:30.052 --> 00:40:33.556
Hitler knows
if we can stand up to him

00:40:33.556 --> 00:40:35.850
all Europe may be freed.

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:41.188
During the Battle of Britain,
when the question whether to commit

00:40:41.188 --> 00:40:45.651
the last reserve
squadrons of fighters to the air

00:40:45.651 --> 00:40:48.821
battle over over Britain,
the Air Force commander

00:40:48.821 --> 00:40:53.409
felt able to do that
because the Enigma intelligence

00:40:53.409 --> 00:40:59.665
had reinforced his own perceptions
about the balance of forces.

00:40:59.665 --> 00:41:01.125
And we all know that

00:41:01.125 --> 00:41:04.128
the outcome of that battle
was a victory for the British

00:41:04.128 --> 00:41:07.047
and a strategic defeat
for the Germans.

00:41:07.047 --> 00:41:10.342
By the end of 1941,
the British had 16 Bombes

00:41:10.342 --> 00:41:13.762
in operation, six at Bletchley
and ten in the nearby towns,

00:41:13.762 --> 00:41:17.266
and they were routinely deciphering
German Army and Luftwaffe

00:41:17.266 --> 00:41:18.392
Enigma traffic.

00:41:18.392 --> 00:41:20.186
This intelligence would soon help

00:41:20.186 --> 00:41:22.646
turn the tide of the war
in North Africa.

00:41:22.646 --> 00:41:23.606
Enigma decrypts

00:41:23.606 --> 00:41:26.233
reveal that the German army
was critically short

00:41:26.233 --> 00:41:28.068
of fuel and supplies.

00:41:28.068 --> 00:41:30.613
Armed with that knowledge,
British General

00:41:30.613 --> 00:41:32.531
Bernard Montgomery launched
the offensive

00:41:32.531 --> 00:41:35.284
that culminated in the victory
at the Second Battle of

00:41:35.284 --> 00:41:38.913
El Alamein in 1942.

00:41:38.913 --> 00:41:40.206
What Turing did

00:41:40.206 --> 00:41:44.460
was so resilient
to protocol changes

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:48.797
that it would have worked
at any time prior to this.

00:41:48.797 --> 00:41:52.092
It is fundamental to the structure
of the Enigma machine,

00:41:52.092 --> 00:41:54.512
and it's so fundamental
that they actually

00:41:54.512 --> 00:41:56.722
ultimately
had to change the machine itself

00:41:56.722 --> 00:41:58.682
in order to try to avoid this,
just to make it

00:41:58.682 --> 00:42:02.520
like marginally more difficult
for them to to continue doing

00:42:02.520 --> 00:42:03.771
by adding a fourth rotor.

00:42:03.771 --> 00:42:06.357
And then even doing
that was a nightmare for them,

00:42:06.357 --> 00:42:09.151
because you have to have it
be back or it's compatible.

00:42:09.151 --> 00:42:09.944
So it's like

00:42:09.944 --> 00:42:12.905
they kind of once Turing did that,
it was like, game over.

00:42:12.905 --> 00:42:15.741
You either
reinvent the entire machine or,

00:42:15.741 --> 00:42:18.994
you know, prevent them from knowing
any information whatsoever.

00:42:18.994 --> 00:42:20.955
The German Navy did just that.

00:42:20.955 --> 00:42:23.666
They added a fourth rotor
to the Naval Enigma machine,

00:42:23.666 --> 00:42:26.794
and they created three extra rotors
to choose from.

00:42:26.794 --> 00:42:28.504
Because of that, Turing
had to invent

00:42:28.504 --> 00:42:30.214
a whole new statistical method

00:42:30.214 --> 00:42:32.216
to narrow
down the choice of rotors,

00:42:32.216 --> 00:42:35.386
and they also had to work
with the Navy to capture key

00:42:35.386 --> 00:42:38.430
sheets and trick the Germans
into revealing a crib.

00:42:38.430 --> 00:42:39.974
And they had to call
in the military

00:42:39.974 --> 00:42:43.936
to try to generate plaintext
for them with gardening.

00:42:43.936 --> 00:42:47.022
Well, sometimes if they were really
struggling to get plaintext, like

00:42:47.022 --> 00:42:50.109
in the case of the blackout
with the Naval Enigma,

00:42:50.109 --> 00:42:52.903
they would basically tell them
to seed mines

00:42:52.903 --> 00:42:55.823
in particular locations,
and then those places would have

00:42:55.823 --> 00:42:56.907
to go and clear them out.

00:42:56.907 --> 00:43:02.121
And so they would
have to say the location.
- Oh that’s genius!

00:43:02.121 --> 00:43:03.247
Yeah.

00:43:03.247 --> 00:43:05.082
Oh, that's really cool.

00:43:05.082 --> 00:43:06.625
But even then, the codebreakers

00:43:06.625 --> 00:43:08.586
relied on the members of the British Navy

00:43:08.586 --> 00:43:10.004
sacrificing their lives

00:43:10.004 --> 00:43:13.382
to capture vital intelligence
from sinking U-boats.

00:43:13.382 --> 00:43:19.346
I am conscious all the time
that codebreaking is not the same

00:43:19.346 --> 00:43:24.268
as combat, and battles are not won
by codebreakers

00:43:24.268 --> 00:43:28.897
sitting in rooms
in the safety of Bletchley Park.

00:43:28.897 --> 00:43:32.693
They're won by people at the sharp
end of things.

00:43:32.693 --> 00:43:33.944
By 1943,

00:43:33.944 --> 00:43:35.362
the Americans had joined the war

00:43:35.362 --> 00:43:38.616
and built
over 100 Bombes of their own.

00:43:38.616 --> 00:43:43.787
You can see the turning point
of the war being when the British

00:43:43.787 --> 00:43:47.082
finally mastered the U-boat Enigma

00:43:47.082 --> 00:43:51.587
in 1943,
the sinkings drop like a stone,

00:43:51.587 --> 00:43:55.215
and instead
the U-boat sinkings start to rise.

00:43:55.215 --> 00:44:00.220
And that's that's the point
at which Germany has begun

00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:00.804
to lose the war.

00:44:00.804 --> 00:44:04.808
I'm not trying to say that Germany
lost the war solely because of the,

00:44:04.808 --> 00:44:06.143
U-boat thing.

00:44:06.143 --> 00:44:09.521
I mean, obviously the Eastern Front
was crucially important too

00:44:09.521 --> 00:44:13.400
but all these things conspired
to mean that basically,

00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:16.695
by the end of 1943,
everybody apart from Adolf

00:44:16.695 --> 00:44:19.239
Hitler knew that the Germans
were going to lose.

00:44:19.239 --> 00:44:23.577
Enigma traffic from all Nazi forces
was now being deciphered daily.

00:44:23.577 --> 00:44:24.578
This set the stage

00:44:24.578 --> 00:44:28.957
for the D-Day landings in 1944
and the Allied invasion of Europe.

00:44:28.957 --> 00:44:32.086
Historians estimate that
the breaking of Enigma shortened

00:44:32.086 --> 00:44:35.839
the war by up to two years, and
who knows how many lives had saved.

00:44:35.839 --> 00:44:38.801
Alan Turing's life story

00:44:38.801 --> 00:44:45.683
is dominated by his sexuality
and what happened to him.

00:44:45.683 --> 00:44:48.560
Bearing in mind
that being a practicing homosexual

00:44:48.560 --> 00:44:52.648
in the UK was still illegal
for the whole of Alan Turing's lifetime.

00:44:52.648 --> 00:44:55.651
That story is very strongly colored
by the fact that Alan Turing

00:44:55.651 --> 00:44:57.611
took his own life.

00:44:57.611 --> 00:45:01.907
That tends to lead to a chain

00:45:01.907 --> 00:45:06.662
of connections in people's minds,
that that means that his whole life

00:45:06.662 --> 00:45:09.873
after World War
II was on a downward spiral.

00:45:09.873 --> 00:45:12.501
I have to say, I disagree.

00:45:12.501 --> 00:45:14.795
From 1945

00:45:14.795 --> 00:45:18.757
through to 1950,
he was involved in the most

00:45:18.757 --> 00:45:22.094
exciting technology projects
that the country had on offer.

00:45:22.094 --> 00:45:23.595
He was very frustrated
from time to time

00:45:23.595 --> 00:45:25.305
because they weren't going fast
enough.

00:45:25.305 --> 00:45:26.390
You know, he was writing programs

00:45:26.390 --> 00:45:28.434
for computers
that weren't yet being built.

00:45:28.434 --> 00:45:31.770
So I think he achieved a great deal
in the postwar years.

00:45:31.770 --> 00:45:36.275
And I have to say,
I think that the end of his life

00:45:36.275 --> 00:45:38.652
does not actually define
what happened before.

00:45:38.652 --> 00:45:42.030
And I think that we're
looking at it the wrong way.

00:45:42.030 --> 00:45:45.534
If we see him
as some sort of martyr.

00:45:45.534 --> 00:45:49.163
In 1944, Allied forces
were preparing to invade Europe.

00:45:49.163 --> 00:45:51.415
But before they did,
Churchill and Roosevelt

00:45:51.415 --> 00:45:53.208
launched one of the most important

00:45:53.208 --> 00:45:55.127
misinformation
campaigns in history.

00:45:55.127 --> 00:45:56.795
They leaked information
to the Nazis

00:45:56.795 --> 00:45:59.465
that they were going to land
on a different beach.

00:45:59.465 --> 00:46:02.384
The deception plan
about where the invasion of Europe

00:46:02.384 --> 00:46:03.093
was going to take place,

00:46:03.093 --> 00:46:07.097
was it going to be in Normandy
or was it going to be

00:46:07.097 --> 00:46:08.265
at the Pas-de-Calais?

00:46:08.265 --> 00:46:11.310
But before
the allies could land at Normandy,

00:46:11.310 --> 00:46:13.270
they needed to know
whether this misinformation

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:14.688
campaign had actually worked.

00:46:14.688 --> 00:46:18.567
And that meant deciphering messages
from Hitler himself.

00:46:18.567 --> 00:46:20.486
Now that the enigma
had been broken.

00:46:20.486 --> 00:46:21.570
That should have been easy.

00:46:21.570 --> 00:46:24.656
But Hitler
actually didn't use Enigma.

00:46:24.656 --> 00:46:28.494
He used a completely different
encryption machine, one

00:46:28.494 --> 00:46:30.078
that the allies had never seen.

00:46:30.078 --> 00:46:33.874
And while Enigma had a key space
of around ten to the power of 23,

00:46:33.874 --> 00:46:38.128
this machine had a key
space of ten to the power of 170.

00:46:38.128 --> 00:46:38.837
That's greater

00:46:38.837 --> 00:46:42.299
than the number of atoms
in the observable universe squared.

00:46:42.299 --> 00:46:42.674
To break

00:46:42.674 --> 00:46:43.425
this would require

00:46:43.425 --> 00:46:44.384
a piece of genius

00:46:44.384 --> 00:46:46.303
that had been described
as the greatest

00:46:46.303 --> 00:46:49.014
intellectual feat of World
War Two, and the building

00:46:49.014 --> 00:46:52.643
of an entirely new machine vastly
more sophisticated than the Bombe.

00:46:52.643 --> 00:46:54.603
Yet you've probably never heard
of the guy

00:46:54.603 --> 00:46:57.397
who built it, or the codebreakers
who made it possible.

00:46:57.397 --> 00:46:59.775
That's because this device
was so important

00:46:59.775 --> 00:47:00.901
during the Cold War

00:47:00.901 --> 00:47:04.321
that it was classified for decades
after the bomb and the Enigma.

00:47:04.321 --> 00:47:07.199
If you want to hear about it.
Let us know in the comments.

00:47:09.701 --> 00:47:12.579
Hey, I just want to give a quick
shout out to Bletchley Park,

00:47:12.579 --> 00:47:14.081
the National Museum of Computing

00:47:14.081 --> 00:47:16.250
and Sir Dermot Turing
and every expert here

00:47:16.250 --> 00:47:18.293
who has helped us
make this video possible.

00:47:18.293 --> 00:47:20.337
If you're interested in visiting,
all the links

00:47:20.337 --> 00:47:22.214
are in the description,
so check that out.

00:47:22.214 --> 00:47:25.300
One last thing, by the way, there's
still a Naval Enigma message

00:47:25.300 --> 00:47:28.846
intercepted in the war that no one
has been able to decrypt.

00:47:28.846 --> 00:47:30.931
The ciphertext
is in the description.

00:47:30.931 --> 00:47:33.433
Maybe you can break
it. Let us know if you can.

